  "body": "Stress-testing the ICT AM continuation strategy across eight instruments and eight years produced a result that had been wrong twice over. Both failure modes are cheap to repeat, neither announces itself, and both are now pinned by tests.\n\n## The two traps\n\n**A feed's timezone is not what the file says, and a flat offset hides the error for half the year.** histdata's ASCII M1 exports are stamped New York local time *with DST*, not the fixed EST they are usually read as. One flat offset is right in January and an hour out in July; since the strategy converts back to an exchange timezone to test its session, that hour moves the entire trading window for eight months of every year. Read that way the strategy measured +39 points over eight years; read correctly, +7.\n\nThe volume profile was ambiguous here and levels are useless (two vendors quoting one index differ by a basis that swamps the comparison). What works is comparing *returns* separately for a winter and a summer month — they align at 0.99 and only at the right offset, and an offset that differs between the two months is DST.\n\n**A result means nothing until it clears the disagreement between two vendors of the same instrument.** The same strategy on the S&P from two feeds gave +50bp and −234bp over the same period, while correlating 0.93 year over year — both agreed about the shape of every year and disagreed about the sign of the total. The gap averaged ~35bp/year against a measured edge of ~6bp/year.\n\n## What this adds\n\n`tools/backtest_lab.py`:\n\n- `to_utc` / `verify_timezone` — DST-correct conversion, and the return-correlation check for identifying an unknown feed's zone. The ambiguous and non-existent DST hours are dropped rather than guessed.\n- `noise_floor` — takes one strategy's per-period results from two vendors and reports whether the edge exceeds the vendor gap. Deliberately one-sided: clearing it is necessary, nowhere near sufficient.\n- `Result.bps` — normalises to basis points of price, so instruments at different quote levels can be compared at all. Summing raw points across instruments produces a number dominated by whichever quote is largest.\n- `backtest` — charges slippage and commission by default, because the strategy that motivated this is gross-positive and net-negative. It passes `mintick` only to a strategy that declares it.\n\n`CLAUDE.md` gains a \"Backtesting\" section covering both traps and the two habits that follow, plus a Layout entry.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `pytest tests/ -v`: 1132 passed, 1 skipped (17 new)\n- `black --check pwb_toolbox/ tools/ tests/`: clean\n- New tests pin the January/July offsets explicitly, the dropped DST hours, left-labelled resampling, bps normalisation, the noise-floor verdict in both directions, and an end-to-end run of a converted PineScript strategy over synthetic bars. Nothing touches the network or market data.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01H67p7LLDE4W1Vg2A3PJngZ\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01H67p7LLDE4W1Vg2A3PJngZ)_",
